1
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:28,160
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,470 --> 00:00:38,303
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:51,450
♪ ♪

4
00:01:05,499 --> 00:01:07,432
♪ ♪

5
00:01:37,031 --> 00:01:38,964
♪ ♪

6
00:02:08,996 --> 00:02:10,929
♪ ♪

7
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:30,169
Hey, what are you doing?

8
00:02:30,217 --> 00:02:31,360
None of your business!

9
00:02:31,384 --> 00:02:32,517
You remember me?

10
00:02:32,586 --> 00:02:33,930
You were looking for
Eddie. Yeah, and you're

11
00:02:33,954 --> 00:02:35,665
the guy who led me
down the primrose path.

12
00:02:35,689 --> 00:02:37,529
This time you're going to talk.

13
00:02:51,038 --> 00:02:54,473
That paper's five days old.

14
00:02:54,541 --> 00:02:57,542
So who's going to notice?

15
00:02:57,611 --> 00:02:59,289
That guy over there might.

16
00:03:06,019 --> 00:03:07,252
Hi.

17
00:03:10,324 --> 00:03:12,001
What the hell do
you think you're doing

18
00:03:12,025 --> 00:03:13,558
running like that?

19
00:03:13,627 --> 00:03:15,927
I had to. My gun was
used to shoot Joseph Chan.

20
00:03:15,996 --> 00:03:17,407
The police would've
thought I did it.

21
00:03:17,431 --> 00:03:18,875
They still think you did it.

22
00:03:18,899 --> 00:03:21,059
Yeah, but at least
this way, I'm not in jail.

23
00:03:21,101 --> 00:03:22,467
Now I know who stole the gun.

24
00:03:22,535 --> 00:03:23,535
I know who shot

25
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:25,137
Mr. Chan and probably
killed the mayor.

26
00:03:25,205 --> 00:03:26,304
Is there a name?

27
00:03:26,373 --> 00:03:27,506
Eddie Lomax.

28
00:03:27,574 --> 00:03:29,019
He flew by charter
plane to a little

29
00:03:29,043 --> 00:03:30,387
hick town in Texas this morning.

30
00:03:30,411 --> 00:03:32,389
How'd you find out?
I went to his hotel.

31
00:03:32,413 --> 00:03:33,456
I gave the desk clerk a twenty

32
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:34,824
to let me, uh, look
at his phone bill.

33
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:37,716
Oh, good.

34
00:03:37,784 --> 00:03:40,051
Now, maybe you
better turn yourself in.

35
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:41,953
I'm going to Texas.

36
00:03:42,022 --> 00:03:43,521
No, no, you're not.

37
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:44,856
I have to.

38
00:03:46,226 --> 00:03:48,626
You're a fugitive.

39
00:03:48,695 --> 00:03:51,296
Every policeman in this
town is looking for you.

40
00:03:51,365 --> 00:03:52,798
I need some cash.

41
00:03:52,866 --> 00:03:54,544
Forget it! Oh, forget it.

42
00:03:54,568 --> 00:03:56,580
I'm not helping you
flee across state lines.

43
00:03:56,604 --> 00:03:57,747
I'm not fleeing. Forget it!

44
00:03:57,771 --> 00:03:58,771
I know what I'm doing.

45
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:01,840
I'll do it if I have to walk.

46
00:04:18,492 --> 00:04:20,370
Given any thought to
what you're going to do

47
00:04:20,394 --> 00:04:21,404
when you find this fella?

48
00:04:21,428 --> 00:04:23,328
Call you.

49
00:04:42,015 --> 00:04:43,592
The police have
probably questioned

50
00:04:43,616 --> 00:04:47,118
everybody around here who
has a view of Chan's house.

51
00:04:47,187 --> 00:04:49,787
Oh, they think Paul
O'Neill's their man.

52
00:04:49,856 --> 00:04:52,002
They're not going to ask
him any questions though.

53
00:04:52,026 --> 00:04:53,591
Blow their case.

54
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:54,792
Well, I am.

55
00:04:54,861 --> 00:04:56,038
Nobody's going to railroad him,

56
00:04:56,062 --> 00:04:58,463
if I can help it.

57
00:04:58,532 --> 00:05:00,532
He likes you, too.

58
00:05:00,601 --> 00:05:01,733
I know.

59
00:05:01,802 --> 00:05:03,780
I think he's got a crush on you.

60
00:05:03,804 --> 00:05:05,404
I know.

61
00:05:06,973 --> 00:05:09,074
How do you know?

62
00:05:09,143 --> 00:05:10,908
I just know.

63
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:12,222
The way he looks at you?

64
00:05:12,246 --> 00:05:13,845
Yeah. Yeah.

65
00:05:15,482 --> 00:05:16,747
Hey, look.

66
00:05:18,651 --> 00:05:21,130
Nobody knows what
goes on in a neighborhood

67
00:05:21,154 --> 00:05:23,714
better than somebody
who never leaves it.

68
00:05:29,096 --> 00:05:30,696
Mrs. Luzinsky.

69
00:05:30,764 --> 00:05:32,263
Thank you so much.

70
00:05:32,333 --> 00:05:33,876
You're such a dear.
You're welcome.

71
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,478
This smells wonderful! Good.

72
00:05:35,502 --> 00:05:37,001
Thank you.

73
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:38,603
Hello.

74
00:05:38,672 --> 00:05:40,037
My name is Ben Matlock

75
00:05:40,106 --> 00:05:41,251
and this is Michelle Thomas.

76
00:05:41,275 --> 00:05:43,319
I wonder if we might
ask you a few questions?

77
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:46,244
Why, certainly!
Come on right in.

78
00:05:49,616 --> 00:05:51,549
There we are. Just
come right in there.

79
00:05:51,618 --> 00:05:53,118
Oh, thank you.

80
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:57,422
Please, sit down.

81
00:05:57,490 --> 00:05:59,490
Thank you. Okay.

82
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:02,293
My name is Sarah Luzinsky.

83
00:06:02,362 --> 00:06:03,461
Good day.

84
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:05,107
Please, eat your
dinner while it's hot.

85
00:06:05,131 --> 00:06:06,197
Oh, thank you.

86
00:06:06,266 --> 00:06:07,933
Are you a police
officer too, dear?

87
00:06:08,001 --> 00:06:09,879
Uh, no, no, I'm an attorney.

88
00:06:09,903 --> 00:06:11,903
We're both attorneys.

89
00:06:11,972 --> 00:06:13,338
You know that the man

90
00:06:13,407 --> 00:06:14,850
across the street was murdered

91
00:06:14,874 --> 00:06:16,786
last night? I know.

92
00:06:16,810 --> 00:06:19,311
Terrible! A terrible thing!

93
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:22,414
And Mr. Chan, such a sweet man.

94
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:25,250
The yard look always so nice.

95
00:06:25,319 --> 00:06:27,096
Of course, he had a gardener.

96
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:28,719
Nice-looking young man.

97
00:06:28,788 --> 00:06:29,987
Very strong.

98
00:06:30,057 --> 00:06:31,523
Very particular.

99
00:06:31,591 --> 00:06:33,324
Pruning all the time.

100
00:06:35,396 --> 00:06:39,597
Well, did you, uh, did
you notice anything...

101
00:06:39,666 --> 00:06:42,100
out of the ordinary last night?

102
00:06:44,470 --> 00:06:49,907
You do... spend
a... a good bit of time

103
00:06:49,976 --> 00:06:52,009
in front of that window.

104
00:06:52,078 --> 00:06:55,413
That's why you thought
we were police officers.

105
00:06:55,482 --> 00:06:59,851
You saw me go in
Mr. Chan's house with them

106
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:01,130
last night, didn't you?

107
00:07:01,154 --> 00:07:02,720
Yes.

108
00:07:02,790 --> 00:07:05,323
What else did you see?

109
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,728
Maybe if I would have
some time to think about it.

110
00:07:09,797 --> 00:07:11,574
Mrs. Luzinsky, it's all right

111
00:07:11,598 --> 00:07:13,231
to get involved.

112
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:15,733
But that's not
what I'm afraid of.

113
00:07:15,802 --> 00:07:18,569
If anybody, for one second...

114
00:07:18,638 --> 00:07:20,171
thought you were in danger,

115
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:22,273
you'd be protected.

116
00:07:22,342 --> 00:07:24,254
You mean people
will stay with me?

117
00:07:24,278 --> 00:07:27,845
Sarah, you tell us what you saw,

118
00:07:27,915 --> 00:07:31,183
I guarantee people
will stay with you,

119
00:07:31,251 --> 00:07:34,452
talk with you, visit with you.

120
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:37,755
Well, first, I saw
a kind of tall man

121
00:07:37,824 --> 00:07:41,326
with long, brown hair,
wearing cowboy boots.

122
00:07:41,394 --> 00:07:43,261
He went into Mr. Chan's house.

123
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:45,563
Then, a little bit later,

124
00:07:45,632 --> 00:07:48,311
another man I didn't
know went in. Uh, this man

125
00:07:48,335 --> 00:07:52,436
with the long, brown
hair and cowboy boots,

126
00:07:52,505 --> 00:07:54,639
did you get a good
look at his face?

127
00:07:54,708 --> 00:07:56,641
Yup.

128
00:07:57,711 --> 00:07:58,843
Look.

129
00:08:00,347 --> 00:08:01,846
See?

130
00:08:01,915 --> 00:08:04,115
Oh...

131
00:08:13,193 --> 00:08:14,726
Why, look at that!

132
00:08:14,795 --> 00:08:17,829
He looks just like
Lee Arthur Greenway.

133
00:08:17,898 --> 00:08:19,998
Oh? I dated him once.

134
00:08:20,067 --> 00:08:21,232
Oh, Lee Arthur?

135
00:08:21,301 --> 00:08:24,002
Yes, when I was in high school.
- Oh...

136
00:08:24,071 --> 00:08:25,336
How's it going?

137
00:08:25,405 --> 00:08:27,138
Oh, well, fine,
Sergeant; thank you.

138
00:08:27,207 --> 00:08:30,508
And this one looks
like Floyd Dinkleman.

139
00:08:30,576 --> 00:08:32,877
Floyd Dinkleman went
to my high school, too.

140
00:08:32,946 --> 00:08:34,245
He threw up a lot.

141
00:08:34,313 --> 00:08:38,750
Oh... Oh, yeah, here.

142
00:08:38,819 --> 00:08:40,085
Yes, sir?

143
00:08:40,153 --> 00:08:42,031
What the hell is
taking her so long?

144
00:08:42,055 --> 00:08:43,387
Well, she doesn't get out much

145
00:08:43,456 --> 00:08:45,023
and it's kind of
fun for her to...

146
00:08:45,092 --> 00:08:46,457
You kill me.

147
00:08:46,526 --> 00:08:48,137
The only guy she saw running

148
00:08:48,161 --> 00:08:49,839
from the scene of
the crime was O'Neill,

149
00:08:49,863 --> 00:08:51,708
and that's the guy
I want to talk to.

150
00:08:51,732 --> 00:08:53,665
There he is! Now, here!

151
00:08:55,836 --> 00:08:57,268
Eddie Lomax.

152
00:08:58,338 --> 00:08:59,638
Eddie Lomax.

153
00:08:59,706 --> 00:09:02,607
That verifies Paul
O'Neill's story.

154
00:09:02,676 --> 00:09:05,777
And in any court of
law, he's off the hook.

155
00:09:07,881 --> 00:09:09,692
Thank you very
much, Mrs. Luzinsky.

156
00:09:09,716 --> 00:09:11,649
You're very welcome, Sergeant.

157
00:09:12,953 --> 00:09:16,154
Well, well, if it
weren't for Chris,

158
00:09:16,222 --> 00:09:17,700
we could be done now. Yeah.

159
00:09:17,724 --> 00:09:19,658
But let's see if Eddie
Lomax has ever

160
00:09:19,726 --> 00:09:22,193
had dealings with
our other suspects.

161
00:09:22,262 --> 00:09:25,062
That is, after we
take Sarah here

162
00:09:25,131 --> 00:09:27,832
for a sarsaparilla and
hear a little more about

163
00:09:27,901 --> 00:09:29,011
Lee Arthur Greenway.

164
00:09:29,035 --> 00:09:33,104
Well, that will take a
double vodka martini.

165
00:09:33,173 --> 00:09:35,073
Oh, my...

166
00:09:38,812 --> 00:09:41,579
Next time, we're sitting
where I can smoke.

167
00:09:41,648 --> 00:09:44,193
There won't be a next time.

168
00:09:44,217 --> 00:09:46,284
Good Lord... What?

169
00:09:47,387 --> 00:09:49,653
Oh, yeah.

170
00:09:51,924 --> 00:09:54,925
Judge Franklin, Mr. Brennert.

171
00:09:54,994 --> 00:09:56,472
Your secretary told
me you'd be here.

172
00:09:56,496 --> 00:09:58,741
I told her it was a
matter of life or death.

173
00:09:59,866 --> 00:10:01,999
But since I've got you

174
00:10:02,068 --> 00:10:03,401
both here, this man's

175
00:10:03,470 --> 00:10:05,803
name is Eddie Lomax...
You recognize him?

176
00:10:05,872 --> 00:10:07,004
Can't help you.

177
00:10:07,073 --> 00:10:08,917
He ever work in
one of your factories?

178
00:10:08,941 --> 00:10:10,040
How would I know?

179
00:10:10,109 --> 00:10:11,909
Ask my personnel manager.

180
00:10:11,979 --> 00:10:13,077
Uh-huh.

181
00:10:13,146 --> 00:10:14,924
How about you, Judge?
Did he ever appear

182
00:10:14,948 --> 00:10:17,581
before you? Not
as far as I know.

183
00:10:17,651 --> 00:10:19,128
Ah. Hey, we're trying to have

184
00:10:19,152 --> 00:10:20,284
dinner here, do you mind?

185
00:10:20,354 --> 00:10:22,453
Oh.

186
00:10:22,522 --> 00:10:27,591
Oh... Janice, please
come sit down.

187
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:29,772
Uh, Mr. Matlock
was just leaving.

188
00:10:29,796 --> 00:10:31,941
Well, perhaps you care
to join us for a drink?

189
00:10:31,965 --> 00:10:33,809
Oh, no thank you.
I have to be going.

190
00:10:33,833 --> 00:10:35,812
I'm sure you all
have a lot to discuss,

191
00:10:35,836 --> 00:10:36,935
so I'll just...

192
00:10:42,041 --> 00:10:43,419
Well, what was he doing here?

193
00:10:43,443 --> 00:10:46,544
Nothing for you to
concern yourself with.

194
00:10:47,647 --> 00:10:49,214
We, uh... we haven't

195
00:10:49,282 --> 00:10:51,682
seen you since
the funeral, Janice.

196
00:10:51,751 --> 00:10:53,084
We were worried about you.

197
00:10:53,153 --> 00:10:54,919
I'm holding up.

198
00:10:54,988 --> 00:10:57,288
Janice, you know

199
00:10:57,357 --> 00:11:01,425
we were Brian's
good friends, and...

200
00:11:01,494 --> 00:11:04,695
so if there's anything
that we can do,

201
00:11:04,764 --> 00:11:06,130
anything that you need...

202
00:11:06,199 --> 00:11:08,039
Thank you. That's very sweet.

203
00:11:10,003 --> 00:11:12,203
Actually, there is
something you can do for me.

204
00:11:16,809 --> 00:11:19,210
I don't know what the
two of you have been up to,

205
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:21,156
but I'm pretty sure that it

206
00:11:21,180 --> 00:11:22,747
cost my husband his life.

207
00:11:22,815 --> 00:11:25,650
And Joseph Chan his.

208
00:11:25,719 --> 00:11:28,886
I'm also pretty sure that
if I took what I do know

209
00:11:28,955 --> 00:11:31,623
to someone like Ben Matlock,

210
00:11:31,691 --> 00:11:34,170
he just might be able to put it

211
00:11:34,194 --> 00:11:36,327
all together.

212
00:11:36,396 --> 00:11:39,497
So this is what you can
do for me, gentlemen.

213
00:11:41,201 --> 00:11:43,334
You can cut me in.

214
00:11:58,852 --> 00:12:00,196
Mr. Reardon! Here he is!

215
00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:01,463
If you're convicted,

216
00:12:01,487 --> 00:12:03,666
do you think it'll
lead to tighter laws?

217
00:12:03,690 --> 00:12:05,101
How did you plan the
murder? How do you feel

218
00:12:05,125 --> 00:12:06,769
about spending the
rest of your life in prison?

219
00:12:06,793 --> 00:12:09,693
Have you sold your
story to TV? Chris!

220
00:12:09,762 --> 00:12:11,674
What do you think your
chances are of parole?

221
00:12:11,698 --> 00:12:12,730
Get away from me!

222
00:12:12,799 --> 00:12:14,699
Are you crazy?!

223
00:12:26,846 --> 00:12:28,757
Now, you come unglued out there

224
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:30,226
and you don't have a prayer.

225
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:31,493
I don't have a prayer anyway.

226
00:12:31,517 --> 00:12:32,895
The whole world's got me tried,

227
00:12:32,919 --> 00:12:34,385
convicted and sentenced.

228
00:12:34,454 --> 00:12:37,255
You lose your temper
with those people,

229
00:12:37,323 --> 00:12:38,800
it's going to be
in the newspaper,

230
00:12:38,824 --> 00:12:40,224
it's going to be on television.

231
00:12:40,293 --> 00:12:42,071
And you're going
to make it real easy

232
00:12:42,095 --> 00:12:43,939
for people to
believe that you are

233
00:12:43,963 --> 00:12:45,429
some kind of raving maniac

234
00:12:45,498 --> 00:12:48,132
who could very well
have shot the mayor.

235
00:12:48,201 --> 00:12:49,545
Now, I'm going to tell you

236
00:12:49,569 --> 00:12:51,870
something: if there's
one thing I hate,

237
00:12:51,938 --> 00:12:54,939
it's losing a case before I
ever walk in the courtroom.

238
00:12:55,008 --> 00:12:56,786
Now, that's going
to be the longest

239
00:12:56,810 --> 00:12:58,620
20 yards you ever walked.

240
00:12:58,644 --> 00:13:00,078
But you got to walk it.

241
00:13:00,147 --> 00:13:02,180
And you got to keep your cool.

242
00:13:02,249 --> 00:13:03,614
You got that?!

243
00:13:05,418 --> 00:13:07,051
Now, turn around and face

244
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:08,964
that mirror, and
repeat after me.

245
00:13:11,525 --> 00:13:13,257
Here he is!

246
00:13:13,326 --> 00:13:14,425
No comments.

247
00:13:14,494 --> 00:13:15,993
We'll have a statement later.

248
00:13:16,062 --> 00:13:17,662
Excuse us, please.

249
00:13:17,730 --> 00:13:19,664
Mr. Reardon, are you
changing your plea?

250
00:13:19,732 --> 00:13:21,412
Do you think you could
kill somebody again?

251
00:13:22,501 --> 00:13:24,647
Do you own many handguns?

252
00:13:24,671 --> 00:13:25,903
Why'd you do it?

253
00:13:25,972 --> 00:13:27,382
Who'd you vote for
in the last election?

254
00:13:27,406 --> 00:13:29,674
Do you plan to plead insanity?

255
00:13:32,011 --> 00:13:33,444
One more question!

256
00:13:34,881 --> 00:13:37,448
♪ ♪

257
00:14:28,567 --> 00:14:30,379
Look what you just did, pinhead!

258
00:14:30,403 --> 00:14:32,636
Now... Whoa!

259
00:14:37,410 --> 00:14:38,942
Hit him!

260
00:14:39,011 --> 00:14:40,731
Is that it? Is that it?

261
00:14:41,847 --> 00:14:43,947
Sit down. Yes, sir.

262
00:14:51,190 --> 00:14:54,337
That's right, I was
Mayor Barelli's press aid.

263
00:14:54,361 --> 00:14:56,927
It was my job to deal
with the news media.

264
00:14:58,030 --> 00:14:59,830
Not only did I organize

265
00:14:59,899 --> 00:15:02,066
the press conference
that morning,

266
00:15:02,135 --> 00:15:05,403
but I was at
Mayor Barelli's side

267
00:15:05,472 --> 00:15:07,672
when he was shot.

268
00:15:07,741 --> 00:15:09,752
You were standing there,
yes, but did you actually

269
00:15:09,776 --> 00:15:11,620
see the person who
shot him, Miss Landis?

270
00:15:11,644 --> 00:15:13,844
Yes.

271
00:15:13,913 --> 00:15:16,814
As the mayor began his
speech, I-I looked around.

272
00:15:16,883 --> 00:15:19,316
You know, to gauge
people's reactions.

273
00:15:19,385 --> 00:15:22,320
I happened to look
toward the mezzanine,

274
00:15:22,389 --> 00:15:24,555
and I saw someone standing there

275
00:15:24,624 --> 00:15:26,557
pointing a gun right at Brian.

276
00:15:26,626 --> 00:15:29,293
A split second
later, the man fired.

277
00:15:29,362 --> 00:15:32,722
Is that man here in this
courtroom today? Yes.

278
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:35,800
He's right there.

279
00:15:35,869 --> 00:15:37,846
That's the man who
killed Mayor Barelli!

280
00:15:37,870 --> 00:15:39,381
That's a lie! No, Chris, please!

281
00:15:39,405 --> 00:15:41,416
The defendant will refrain
from making any comments.

282
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:42,985
This is nuts! Am I really
supposed to sit here

283
00:15:43,009 --> 00:15:44,920
while people lie
about me? This is nuts!

284
00:15:44,944 --> 00:15:46,221
Sit down immediately!
Why the hell

285
00:15:46,245 --> 00:15:47,890
don't you make her tell
the truth?! Your Honor,

286
00:15:47,914 --> 00:15:50,192
Order! if I might just have
a word with my client?

287
00:15:50,216 --> 00:15:51,794
It's a conspiracy! I will
have order! Mr. Matlock...

288
00:15:51,818 --> 00:15:54,051
You're all part of it! Sit down!

289
00:15:54,120 --> 00:15:55,953
Control your client.

290
00:15:56,022 --> 00:15:59,190
Are you or are you not going

291
00:15:59,259 --> 00:16:01,959
to yield to the
authority of this court,

292
00:16:02,028 --> 00:16:03,995
and comport yourself properly

293
00:16:04,064 --> 00:16:06,142
from here on
out? No, I will not!

294
00:16:06,166 --> 00:16:07,831
Yes, yes, yes! I will not! Stop!

295
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:10,101
I have no choice but

296
00:16:10,170 --> 00:16:12,336
to deal with you the
way I've always dealt

297
00:16:12,404 --> 00:16:14,304
with people like you.

298
00:16:14,373 --> 00:16:16,741
Bailiff!

299
00:16:24,317 --> 00:16:26,317
Prosecution may continue.

300
00:16:26,353 --> 00:16:27,896
Let the record show
that Miss Landis

301
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:29,531
has identified the
defendant as the man

302
00:16:29,555 --> 00:16:31,567
she saw shoot
Mayor Brian Barelli.

303
00:16:31,591 --> 00:16:32,923
I have no further questions.

304
00:16:32,992 --> 00:16:35,204
Defense wish to cross?

305
00:16:35,228 --> 00:16:37,161
Yes, Your Honor.

306
00:16:38,765 --> 00:16:45,235
Before I cross, I feel compelled
to say that while His Honor

307
00:16:45,305 --> 00:16:48,205
is within his rights,

308
00:16:48,274 --> 00:16:50,742
in over 30 years of practice,

309
00:16:50,810 --> 00:16:53,410
I have never seen
anything to equal this.

310
00:16:53,479 --> 00:16:56,313
Mr. Matlock, I've
dealt with your client.

311
00:16:56,383 --> 00:16:59,049
Don't force me to deal with you.

312
00:16:59,118 --> 00:17:00,417
Of course, Your Honor.

313
00:17:00,486 --> 00:17:03,487
Having made my statement
that in 30 years I've never seen

314
00:17:03,556 --> 00:17:04,789
anything to equal this

315
00:17:04,857 --> 00:17:07,157
and knowing it's on the
record, I will move on.

316
00:17:07,227 --> 00:17:09,694
Mr. Matlock!

317
00:17:09,762 --> 00:17:12,496
With the court's permission,

318
00:17:12,565 --> 00:17:15,700
I would like to re-create
some of the conditions

319
00:17:15,768 --> 00:17:20,971
that existed in the
rotunda that morning.

320
00:17:21,041 --> 00:17:24,408
Theatrics, Mr. Matlock?

321
00:17:24,477 --> 00:17:26,143
Theatrics?

322
00:17:26,212 --> 00:17:27,511
Mr. Matlock...

323
00:17:27,580 --> 00:17:31,915
Your Honor, I need
to make a point here,

324
00:17:31,984 --> 00:17:37,021
and I need these
theatrics to make that point.

325
00:17:37,089 --> 00:17:40,391
Proceed.

326
00:17:40,459 --> 00:17:44,896
Uh, Miss Landis, uh, what's
a press conference like?

327
00:17:44,964 --> 00:17:47,064
It's very hectic.

328
00:17:47,133 --> 00:17:49,566
Reporters running
around all over the place,

329
00:17:49,635 --> 00:17:51,769
cameras, microphones, cables.

330
00:17:51,838 --> 00:17:53,337
Lights?

331
00:17:53,405 --> 00:17:57,274
Oh, yes, you can't have TV
and film coverage without lights.

332
00:17:57,343 --> 00:17:59,421
Let me, let me explain
what I've done here.

333
00:17:59,445 --> 00:18:01,523
I've asked these gentlemen
from the TV station

334
00:18:01,547 --> 00:18:03,747
to come down and set up lights

335
00:18:03,817 --> 00:18:06,684
the way they were at
the rotunda that morning.

336
00:18:06,753 --> 00:18:09,820
Now, uh, you were by the mayor

337
00:18:09,889 --> 00:18:12,589
while he was making his speech,

338
00:18:12,658 --> 00:18:14,792
so let's pretend he's
making his speech, all right?

339
00:18:14,861 --> 00:18:16,393
All right.

340
00:18:16,463 --> 00:18:19,764
Now, the lights were
on you as they are now...

341
00:18:19,833 --> 00:18:22,833
Uh, well... Approximately.

342
00:18:22,902 --> 00:18:24,780
You had about this
much light on you.

343
00:18:24,804 --> 00:18:26,904
Well, yes, I guess it
was about this much.

344
00:18:26,973 --> 00:18:32,443
Of course, uh... conditions
are not exactly as they were

345
00:18:32,512 --> 00:18:34,077
in the rotunda that morning.

346
00:18:34,146 --> 00:18:37,381
The man you saw
fire the shot was

347
00:18:37,450 --> 00:18:40,951
much farther away
from you than I am now.

348
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:43,821
And you had to look

349
00:18:43,890 --> 00:18:47,792
above the lights rather than
below them, is that right?

350
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:51,362
Uh, yes. Yes, that's right.

351
00:18:51,431 --> 00:18:52,829
But you can still see me?

352
00:18:52,899 --> 00:18:54,532
Yes.

353
00:18:54,600 --> 00:18:56,601
Can you see me well
enough to point to me?

354
00:18:56,669 --> 00:18:57,835
Sure.

355
00:18:57,904 --> 00:19:00,371
Give me a good strong point.

356
00:19:06,345 --> 00:19:10,748
You didn't really
get a good look

357
00:19:10,816 --> 00:19:14,184
at the man who fired
the shot, did you?

358
00:19:30,069 --> 00:19:31,735
No.

359
00:19:31,804 --> 00:19:38,342
You saw someone<i>
dressed</i> like Chris Reardon.

360
00:19:38,411 --> 00:19:42,879
And in your desire to
see someone punished

361
00:19:42,949 --> 00:19:45,816
for bringing
Brian Barelli's life

362
00:19:45,885 --> 00:19:48,352
to such a sudden and tragic end,

363
00:19:48,421 --> 00:19:51,655
you rushed to a
judgment, didn't you?

364
00:19:53,992 --> 00:19:54,992
Yes.

365
00:20:01,900 --> 00:20:03,100
I'm sorry.

366
00:20:07,006 --> 00:20:09,373
Thank you.

367
00:20:09,442 --> 00:20:11,308
Nothing further.

368
00:20:20,252 --> 00:20:23,321
Hiya. Uh, my
name's Paul O'Neill.

369
00:20:23,389 --> 00:20:25,623
Listen, there's a guy
in the bar over there

370
00:20:25,692 --> 00:20:26,769
who's wanted for questioning

371
00:20:26,793 --> 00:20:28,504
in a murder
investigation in Illinois.

372
00:20:28,528 --> 00:20:31,295
The hell you say? Who?

373
00:20:31,364 --> 00:20:32,663
Eddie Lomax.

374
00:20:32,732 --> 00:20:35,799
I find that kind
of hard to believe.

375
00:20:35,868 --> 00:20:38,269
Eddie, uh... I've
known Eddie for years.

376
00:20:38,338 --> 00:20:41,872
Catching his fair share
of scraps but, uh, murder?

377
00:20:41,941 --> 00:20:43,841
Are you a cop?

378
00:20:43,909 --> 00:20:45,142
No.

379
00:20:45,211 --> 00:20:47,310
You got a writ of extradition?

380
00:20:47,380 --> 00:20:49,312
I thought<i> you</i> might call.

381
00:20:49,382 --> 00:20:52,549
I'm afraid I can't do that.

382
00:20:52,619 --> 00:20:54,986
Not on your word.

383
00:20:55,054 --> 00:20:57,254
You can't just let him go free.

384
00:20:57,323 --> 00:20:59,363
Until he breaks a
law in my jurisdiction,

385
00:20:59,392 --> 00:21:02,359
I sure as hell can.

386
00:21:11,704 --> 00:21:13,349
Hi. Brenda, isn't it?

387
00:21:13,373 --> 00:21:16,340
You know you almost got me
fired the last time you were here,

388
00:21:16,408 --> 00:21:18,743
did you know that,
Mr. Matlock? Oh, I'm sorry,

389
00:21:18,811 --> 00:21:20,689
but you must be doing
something right, if you're still here.

390
00:21:20,713 --> 00:21:21,779
Mr. Brennert is busy.

391
00:21:21,847 --> 00:21:23,726
I just want to ask him
a couple questions

392
00:21:23,750 --> 00:21:24,750
about Judge Franklin.

393
00:21:40,566 --> 00:21:42,166
Where the hell did he go?!

394
00:21:42,235 --> 00:21:44,401
When I see him, I'll ask him.

395
00:22:15,401 --> 00:22:17,467
♪ ♪

396
00:22:46,298 --> 00:22:48,265
♪ ♪

397
00:23:14,827 --> 00:23:16,894
♪ ♪

398
00:23:45,958 --> 00:23:48,992
♪ ♪

399
00:24:22,961 --> 00:24:26,497
♪ ♪

400
00:25:27,659 --> 00:25:29,126
Howdy.

401
00:25:47,747 --> 00:25:49,446
Hey.

402
00:25:49,515 --> 00:25:51,448
You remember me?

403
00:25:53,385 --> 00:25:56,085
What the hell are
you doing here?

404
00:25:56,155 --> 00:25:57,866
Well, I just wanted
to thank you.

405
00:25:57,890 --> 00:26:00,090
You know, for cracking
my head open in that alley,

406
00:26:00,159 --> 00:26:01,691
stealing my gun,

407
00:26:01,760 --> 00:26:04,041
framing me for Chan's
murder, shooting Mayor Barelli.

408
00:26:06,198 --> 00:26:09,666
Actually, uh... I'm
doing a poll of killers

409
00:26:09,735 --> 00:26:12,280
and I thought I
should start with you.

410
00:26:12,304 --> 00:26:15,205
What did you call me?

411
00:26:15,274 --> 00:26:17,394
A murderer. You should
be sent to the chair.

412
00:26:22,914 --> 00:26:24,081
That's gotta hurt.

413
00:26:27,018 --> 00:26:29,058
I'm gonna rip your heart out.

414
00:26:40,600 --> 00:26:42,766
Oh, it's about time.

415
00:26:42,835 --> 00:26:44,969
Arrest this man...
Assault and battery.

416
00:26:45,037 --> 00:26:47,004
Hey, it was self-defense.

417
00:26:47,073 --> 00:26:48,538
He hit me first.

418
00:26:48,608 --> 00:26:53,176
Come on... who do
you think hit whom?

419
00:27:19,405 --> 00:27:21,071
Coming! I'm coming!

420
00:27:25,677 --> 00:27:28,044
Mr. Montero?

421
00:27:28,113 --> 00:27:29,445
Yeah.

422
00:27:29,514 --> 00:27:31,192
Good morning. Here's your paper.

423
00:27:31,216 --> 00:27:32,883
Interesting article

424
00:27:32,952 --> 00:27:37,121
on page two about that warehouse
that blew up a couple days ago.

425
00:27:37,189 --> 00:27:38,955
Who are you?

426
00:27:39,025 --> 00:27:41,324
My name's Ben
Matlock. I'm a lawyer.

427
00:27:43,262 --> 00:27:45,361
I went to see your boss

428
00:27:45,430 --> 00:27:46,730
Jack Brennert the other day,

429
00:27:46,799 --> 00:27:49,432
but he didn't want to
see me, so he slipped out.

430
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:51,635
But my associate followed him.

431
00:27:51,703 --> 00:27:52,903
And guess what?

432
00:27:52,972 --> 00:27:54,204
What?

433
00:27:54,273 --> 00:27:58,275
My associate saw him
slip you an attaché case.

434
00:27:58,343 --> 00:27:59,921
He figured there was
some money in it...

435
00:27:59,945 --> 00:28:03,146
A payoff or something,
so he followed you.

436
00:28:03,215 --> 00:28:04,381
He did what?

437
00:28:04,450 --> 00:28:05,450
Yeah.

438
00:28:05,518 --> 00:28:07,518
Followed you to that warehouse,

439
00:28:07,586 --> 00:28:10,721
watched you put the
attaché case down on a desk,

440
00:28:10,789 --> 00:28:12,188
and then watched you leave.

441
00:28:12,257 --> 00:28:14,535
And then you know what happened?

442
00:28:14,559 --> 00:28:15,839
Damn thing blew up.

443
00:28:15,894 --> 00:28:17,427
Big explosion.

444
00:28:17,496 --> 00:28:18,628
Startled Tyler.

445
00:28:18,697 --> 00:28:22,399
So what do you want?

446
00:28:22,467 --> 00:28:25,168
I want you in court today.

447
00:28:25,237 --> 00:28:26,436
Forget it.

448
00:28:26,504 --> 00:28:28,939
Get your foot out of my
door before I call the cops.

449
00:28:29,007 --> 00:28:31,608
Please, call 'em.

450
00:28:31,677 --> 00:28:33,710
I'll show them this subpoena...

451
00:28:33,779 --> 00:28:35,991
They'll put your heinie in
the back seat of that patrol car

452
00:28:36,015 --> 00:28:37,095
and you'll be in court.

453
00:28:38,684 --> 00:28:40,416
Ready?

454
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:43,553
Yeah.

455
00:28:47,460 --> 00:28:50,193
Mr. Brennert, you have
widespread real estate holdings

456
00:28:50,262 --> 00:28:51,561
in Chicago, don't you?

457
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:53,063
That's true.

458
00:28:53,132 --> 00:28:55,566
According to the official deed

459
00:28:55,634 --> 00:28:56,867
recorded at the courthouse,

460
00:28:56,936 --> 00:29:00,938
you have purchased
ten... uh, industrial parcels

461
00:29:01,006 --> 00:29:03,440
in the last six
months, is that right?

462
00:29:03,509 --> 00:29:04,774
Quite true.

463
00:29:04,843 --> 00:29:06,743
Why all this rush to buy

464
00:29:06,812 --> 00:29:08,412
West Side industrial properties?

465
00:29:08,480 --> 00:29:10,680
Real estate
speculation, Mr., Matlock.

466
00:29:10,749 --> 00:29:12,660
By low, sell high.
It's very simple.

467
00:29:12,684 --> 00:29:17,154
You wouldn't by any chance
be pressuring these businessmen

468
00:29:17,223 --> 00:29:18,322
into selling would you?

469
00:29:18,390 --> 00:29:21,257
I always offer a
high market value.

470
00:29:21,326 --> 00:29:23,360
Huh.

471
00:29:23,429 --> 00:29:26,896
And this, uh, rash of fires

472
00:29:26,966 --> 00:29:28,698
on the West Side recently,

473
00:29:28,767 --> 00:29:30,378
you wouldn't know anything
about that, would you?

474
00:29:30,402 --> 00:29:31,445
In business,

475
00:29:31,469 --> 00:29:33,148
you take advantage
of the breaks.

476
00:29:33,172 --> 00:29:34,738
Or make your own.

477
00:29:34,806 --> 00:29:36,417
Now, what is that,
an accusation?

478
00:29:38,344 --> 00:29:42,179
Your Honor, I have
subpoenaed another witness

479
00:29:42,248 --> 00:29:44,647
to testify this morning
and I'd like him removed

480
00:29:44,716 --> 00:29:46,449
from the courtroom at this time,

481
00:29:46,518 --> 00:29:48,296
so that he won't be
influenced by anything

482
00:29:48,320 --> 00:29:50,653
Mr. Brennert may or may not say.

483
00:29:50,723 --> 00:29:52,089
Very well.

484
00:29:52,157 --> 00:29:54,035
Where is this witness?
He's right back there.

485
00:29:54,059 --> 00:29:56,593
Mr. Thomas Montero.

486
00:29:58,297 --> 00:30:00,857
Bailiff.

487
00:30:05,204 --> 00:30:07,437
Tom Montero works
for you, doesn't he?

488
00:30:07,506 --> 00:30:10,440
Yes.

489
00:30:10,509 --> 00:30:12,542
He's an arsonist, isn't he?

490
00:30:12,611 --> 00:30:13,955
Objection! Relevancy,
no foundation.

491
00:30:13,979 --> 00:30:15,357
He torches building
so that you can go in

492
00:30:15,381 --> 00:30:18,081
later and buy 'em up
cheaply, doesn't he?

493
00:30:18,150 --> 00:30:19,360
That's a lie.

494
00:30:19,384 --> 00:30:20,316
Objection! Relevancy!

495
00:30:20,385 --> 00:30:21,651
Mr. Matlock...

496
00:30:21,720 --> 00:30:25,355
Your Honor, in a moment,
I'll show relevancy.

497
00:30:25,424 --> 00:30:26,923
Proceed.

498
00:30:26,992 --> 00:30:30,961
Now, Mr. Brennert,
the penalty for perjury

499
00:30:31,029 --> 00:30:33,729
in this state is severe, so
I'm going to give you a chance

500
00:30:33,798 --> 00:30:36,732
to reconsider your testimony.

501
00:30:36,801 --> 00:30:38,335
And I remind you

502
00:30:38,403 --> 00:30:40,736
that Mr. Montero is
just outside that door.

503
00:30:40,806 --> 00:30:42,438
Now we'll get the
truth out of you,

504
00:30:42,507 --> 00:30:44,207
or we'll get it out of him.

505
00:30:44,276 --> 00:30:47,510
Now did you or did
you not provide him

506
00:30:47,579 --> 00:30:51,248
with incendiary devices
that he used to burn

507
00:30:51,316 --> 00:30:53,261
one or more buildings
on the West Side?

508
00:30:53,285 --> 00:30:54,784
I did not.

509
00:30:54,853 --> 00:30:56,886
And if he says
I did, he's a liar.

510
00:30:56,955 --> 00:30:59,222
Why did you want those
properties so badly?

511
00:30:59,291 --> 00:31:00,724
Just tell us.

512
00:31:00,792 --> 00:31:02,091
Read my lips, will ya?

513
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:03,926
Real estate speculation.

514
00:31:03,995 --> 00:31:05,862
Speculation.

515
00:31:05,930 --> 00:31:09,366
That's when you buy
property without knowing

516
00:31:09,434 --> 00:31:13,303
whether its value
will go up or down.

517
00:31:13,372 --> 00:31:15,405
But you knew, didn't you?

518
00:31:15,474 --> 00:31:18,775
And I know something, too.

519
00:31:18,843 --> 00:31:21,077
You're not going to
get away with this.

520
00:31:21,146 --> 00:31:22,846
I'm gonna break your heart.

521
00:31:22,915 --> 00:31:26,283
There was no real
speculation, was there?

522
00:31:26,352 --> 00:31:28,618
You had inside information

523
00:31:28,687 --> 00:31:31,888
from Mayor Barelli.

524
00:31:31,957 --> 00:31:33,990
Which properties
on the West Side

525
00:31:34,059 --> 00:31:35,826
would be condemned by the city

526
00:31:35,894 --> 00:31:37,393
for the purpose of building

527
00:31:37,462 --> 00:31:39,729
the new inner city
expressway, didn't you?

528
00:31:44,069 --> 00:31:46,369
Yes, it's true, but I
didn't kill anybody.

529
00:31:46,438 --> 00:31:48,338
One thing at a time.

530
00:31:48,407 --> 00:31:50,252
You knew the route
of the expressway?

531
00:31:50,276 --> 00:31:51,474
Yes, I did.

532
00:31:51,543 --> 00:31:52,609
And that's why

533
00:31:52,677 --> 00:31:56,446
you were so anxious
to get those properties,

534
00:31:56,514 --> 00:31:59,048
because you knew the city would
then have to buy them from you.

535
00:31:59,117 --> 00:32:01,084
That's right.

536
00:32:01,153 --> 00:32:03,753
And the mayor found out
about what you were doing,

537
00:32:03,822 --> 00:32:06,456
and that's when he
accused you of betraying him,

538
00:32:06,525 --> 00:32:08,436
and that's why
he had to be killed.

539
00:32:08,460 --> 00:32:10,460
I didn't kill anybody.

540
00:32:12,497 --> 00:32:14,497
Stuart Franklin
and Janice Barelli...

541
00:32:14,566 --> 00:32:15,966
They were my partners.

542
00:32:16,035 --> 00:32:18,768
Don't ask me who
killed Brian, ask them.

543
00:32:18,837 --> 00:32:20,003
Maybe I will.

544
00:32:21,840 --> 00:32:23,673
Uh, a moment, Your Honor.

545
00:32:28,346 --> 00:32:30,725
Eddie Lomax is
now in the custody

546
00:32:30,749 --> 00:32:32,527
of the Chicago
police department.

547
00:32:32,551 --> 00:32:36,686
Uh, Your Honor, may
counsel approach the bench?

548
00:32:36,755 --> 00:32:38,132
Mr. Price.

549
00:32:38,156 --> 00:32:40,790
You must have really given
them an earful, huh, Chris?

550
00:32:42,161 --> 00:32:44,794
Your Honor, I'd like to
request an adjournment

551
00:32:44,863 --> 00:32:46,296
until tomorrow morning.

552
00:32:46,365 --> 00:32:50,567
I need time to interview a
new and possibly vital witness.

553
00:32:53,705 --> 00:32:57,040
Who hired you to
kill Joseph Chan?

554
00:32:57,108 --> 00:32:58,620
I don't know what
you're talking about.

555
00:32:58,644 --> 00:33:01,055
The same person who
hired you to kill Mayor Barelli?

556
00:33:01,079 --> 00:33:03,780
I still don't know what
you're talking about.

557
00:33:03,849 --> 00:33:06,683
We can place you at
Joseph Chan's murder.

558
00:33:06,752 --> 00:33:08,084
Big deal.

559
00:33:08,153 --> 00:33:09,619
A smart man

560
00:33:09,688 --> 00:33:11,433
would make it easy
on himself, Eddie.

561
00:33:11,457 --> 00:33:12,889
This is boring.

562
00:33:12,958 --> 00:33:15,191
And I'm missing dinner.

563
00:33:15,260 --> 00:33:18,428
So, when you've got something...

564
00:33:18,496 --> 00:33:20,263
you give me a call.

565
00:33:22,167 --> 00:33:23,866
Guard.

566
00:33:36,615 --> 00:33:38,615
Hello, Eddie.

567
00:33:38,684 --> 00:33:40,016
Jimmy.

568
00:33:40,085 --> 00:33:41,017
What's going on?

569
00:33:41,086 --> 00:33:43,086
Nothing much.

570
00:34:09,781 --> 00:34:11,648
You're the one
killed Eddie Lomax?

571
00:34:11,717 --> 00:34:12,993
It was self-defense.

572
00:34:13,017 --> 00:34:14,183
Tell me what happened.

573
00:34:14,252 --> 00:34:15,930
They put me in a cell with him.

574
00:34:15,954 --> 00:34:18,232
Next thing I know, he's
starting to swing at me.

575
00:34:18,256 --> 00:34:20,857
So I duck... he keeps
coming after me.

576
00:34:20,925 --> 00:34:22,458
So, I, uh, I swing at him.

577
00:34:22,527 --> 00:34:24,294
Only I connect.

578
00:34:24,362 --> 00:34:26,562
Then I see he's got
a shiv in his hand.

579
00:34:26,631 --> 00:34:27,797
He comes at me.

580
00:34:27,866 --> 00:34:29,146
I grab it.

581
00:34:29,201 --> 00:34:30,733
He knocks me around.

582
00:34:30,803 --> 00:34:32,280
We roll around
the floor wrestling,

583
00:34:32,304 --> 00:34:36,706
and, uh... the next
thing I know, it's, uh,

584
00:34:36,775 --> 00:34:38,608
sticking out of his chest.

585
00:34:38,677 --> 00:34:40,354
He started it? You bet.

586
00:34:40,378 --> 00:34:41,878
Did you know one another?

587
00:34:41,947 --> 00:34:44,648
Never saw him before in my life.

588
00:34:44,716 --> 00:34:45,716
Stand up.

589
00:34:49,488 --> 00:34:53,256
Eddie's right hand was
broken in two places.

590
00:34:53,325 --> 00:34:55,191
His arm was in a sling.

591
00:34:55,260 --> 00:34:57,527
But he started a fight
with a total stranger

592
00:34:57,595 --> 00:34:59,962
who was a foot taller and
50 pounds heavier than him,

593
00:35:00,032 --> 00:35:01,197
is that it?

594
00:35:01,266 --> 00:35:02,899
That's what happened.

595
00:35:14,079 --> 00:35:15,790
There's nothing in
here on Jimmy Dolan.

596
00:35:15,814 --> 00:35:18,080
Mm. And so much for the
clippings on Jack Brennert.

597
00:35:18,149 --> 00:35:20,517
Eh, now what?

598
00:35:20,585 --> 00:35:22,819
I don't know.

599
00:35:27,492 --> 00:35:29,592
But I am starving.

600
00:35:35,834 --> 00:35:38,312
You know, it's taken
everything I have

601
00:35:38,336 --> 00:35:40,896
not to make a pass at you.

602
00:35:42,240 --> 00:35:45,174
I'll take that as a compliment.

603
00:35:45,243 --> 00:35:47,944
Maybe you'll stick
around after the case?

604
00:35:48,013 --> 00:35:51,647
That could be tomorrow,
if we don't find something.

605
00:35:51,717 --> 00:35:53,482
Hey, look at this.

606
00:35:53,551 --> 00:35:54,751
What is it?

607
00:35:54,820 --> 00:35:56,753
Jimmy Dolan's rap sheet.

608
00:35:56,822 --> 00:35:58,755
That's a lot of arrests.

609
00:35:58,824 --> 00:36:00,802
37. There's no convictions.

610
00:36:00,826 --> 00:36:04,561
Doesn't even look like
he's ever been to trial.

611
00:36:04,630 --> 00:36:06,729
Tell you anything?

612
00:36:11,603 --> 00:36:15,271
Your Honor, before we begin
and before the jury enters,

613
00:36:15,339 --> 00:36:20,276
my client would like to make
a brief personal statement.

614
00:36:20,344 --> 00:36:22,078
Very well.

615
00:36:22,146 --> 00:36:23,846
Bailiff?

616
00:36:29,087 --> 00:36:32,288
I'm sorry I behaved
so disrespectfully.

617
00:36:32,357 --> 00:36:34,290
It's something I regret,

618
00:36:34,359 --> 00:36:35,669
and it won't happen again.

619
00:36:35,693 --> 00:36:38,039
Bailiff, remove the restraints,

620
00:36:38,063 --> 00:36:41,630
and then bring in the jury.

621
00:36:41,699 --> 00:36:44,434
I hope that wasn't just a speech

622
00:36:44,502 --> 00:36:46,936
your attorneys had you
memorize, Mr. Reardon.

623
00:36:47,005 --> 00:36:50,640
If you disrupt these
proceedings again,

624
00:36:50,709 --> 00:36:53,776
I'll throw all of you in
jail for contempt of court.

625
00:36:54,913 --> 00:36:57,880
Yes, sir.

626
00:36:57,949 --> 00:37:01,484
At this time, defense
would like to call...

627
00:37:01,553 --> 00:37:04,521
Judge Stuart
Franklin to the stand.

628
00:37:06,191 --> 00:37:09,659
I understand you
and Mayor Barelli

629
00:37:09,728 --> 00:37:12,362
were old friends.

630
00:37:12,431 --> 00:37:15,998
His ghetto was across
the alley from mine.

631
00:37:16,067 --> 00:37:18,000
We grew up together.

632
00:37:18,069 --> 00:37:20,047
Did you go to school together?

633
00:37:20,071 --> 00:37:21,604
Uh, through high school,

634
00:37:21,673 --> 00:37:25,175
and then he went on to college
and I joined the police force.

635
00:37:25,243 --> 00:37:26,887
And that gave
you the opportunity

636
00:37:26,911 --> 00:37:31,714
to go to night school and
eventually earn a law degree.

637
00:37:31,783 --> 00:37:33,182
That's correct.

638
00:37:33,251 --> 00:37:36,664
You're a real bootstrapped
man, Judge Franklin.

639
00:37:36,688 --> 00:37:38,688
Voters like that.

640
00:37:38,757 --> 00:37:41,624
It's no wonder that you
were elected to the city council

641
00:37:41,693 --> 00:37:43,359
by such a landslide.

642
00:37:43,427 --> 00:37:46,963
And Brian Barelli was on the
council with you, wasn't he?

643
00:37:47,032 --> 00:37:48,531
Yes, he was.

644
00:37:48,599 --> 00:37:52,835
Two old friends
serving side by side.

645
00:37:52,904 --> 00:37:56,973
Must've been quite a shock
when he stabbed you in the back.

646
00:37:58,343 --> 00:37:59,942
He did no such thing.

647
00:38:00,011 --> 00:38:02,390
But, Judge Franklin,
it's-it's common knowledge

648
00:38:02,414 --> 00:38:05,615
that Brian Barelli convinced you

649
00:38:05,683 --> 00:38:08,951
to take the judgeship
which you now hold.

650
00:38:09,020 --> 00:38:10,831
And with you out of the running,

651
00:38:10,855 --> 00:38:14,035
the leaders of the
political party at the time

652
00:38:14,059 --> 00:38:16,959
turned to him as<i> the</i> candidate.

653
00:38:17,028 --> 00:38:19,695
That's warmed-over,
backroom gossip.

654
00:38:19,764 --> 00:38:22,898
He destroyed your chances
of ever being mayor of this city,

655
00:38:22,967 --> 00:38:24,887
and you never
forgave him, did you?

656
00:38:24,936 --> 00:38:26,369
That is not true.

657
00:38:26,437 --> 00:38:29,338
Oh, I know you said
over and over again,

658
00:38:29,407 --> 00:38:32,475
what old and dear
friends you were.

659
00:38:32,544 --> 00:38:35,778
And, uh, you didn't hesitate

660
00:38:35,847 --> 00:38:38,781
to use that friendship, did you?

661
00:38:40,852 --> 00:38:42,852
For inside information.

662
00:38:42,921 --> 00:38:45,622
Information that
could do two things:

663
00:38:45,690 --> 00:38:47,357
make you a lot of money,

664
00:38:47,425 --> 00:38:50,660
and maybe, somewhere
down the line,

665
00:38:50,729 --> 00:38:54,864
destroy the political
career of your old friend.

666
00:38:54,933 --> 00:38:56,933
What you've said is not true.

667
00:38:57,002 --> 00:38:59,935
Do you deny...

668
00:39:00,004 --> 00:39:04,106
that... you were a
member of a plan

669
00:39:04,175 --> 00:39:08,478
to buy up industrial
West Side property?

670
00:39:08,546 --> 00:39:11,213
The only property
I own in this city

671
00:39:11,282 --> 00:39:13,950
is my home. Judge Franklin,

672
00:39:14,018 --> 00:39:16,986
as a jurist, you know full well
you did not answer my question.

673
00:39:17,054 --> 00:39:19,199
And you, sir, have not
introduced any evidence

674
00:39:19,223 --> 00:39:21,824
to prove I was a member
of this conspiracy.

675
00:39:21,893 --> 00:39:24,093
A simple yes or no will do.

676
00:39:24,162 --> 00:39:26,295
Were you a member
of a conspiracy

677
00:39:26,364 --> 00:39:28,698
to purchase West Side property?

678
00:39:28,767 --> 00:39:31,067
No, I was not. But you did know

679
00:39:31,136 --> 00:39:34,437
of the plan the city
had to build a new road.

680
00:39:34,506 --> 00:39:35,237
Of course.

681
00:39:35,306 --> 00:39:37,339
And you discussed
the route with the mayor.

682
00:39:37,408 --> 00:39:38,952
I may have; I don't recall.

683
00:39:38,976 --> 00:39:41,088
I fail to see the point
of all this, Your Honor.

684
00:39:41,112 --> 00:39:43,212
Your Honor, I'll make
the point right now.

685
00:39:43,281 --> 00:39:44,580
Please do.

686
00:39:44,649 --> 00:39:46,849
Immediately. Thanks. Mm-hmm.

687
00:39:48,887 --> 00:39:51,320
Please tell the
court what this is.

688
00:39:54,526 --> 00:39:56,058
A check.

689
00:39:56,127 --> 00:39:57,627
Made out in what amount?

690
00:39:57,695 --> 00:40:01,063
$150,000.

691
00:40:01,132 --> 00:40:03,800
Made out to whom?

692
00:40:03,869 --> 00:40:06,169
Janice Barelli.

693
00:40:06,237 --> 00:40:08,337
Made up<i> by</i> whom?

694
00:40:10,341 --> 00:40:11,808
Me.

695
00:40:11,876 --> 00:40:14,243
Yeah. Yeah.

696
00:40:16,281 --> 00:40:20,883
Now, you may wonder what
I'm doing with this check.

697
00:40:22,821 --> 00:40:24,720
Well, I'll tell you.

698
00:40:24,789 --> 00:40:27,156
Janice Barelli gave it to me...

699
00:40:27,225 --> 00:40:30,426
right after she convinced you

700
00:40:30,495 --> 00:40:32,472
that a three-way conspiracy

701
00:40:32,496 --> 00:40:34,597
would be better
than going to jail.

702
00:40:34,666 --> 00:40:37,466
'Cause, you see, she was
actually conspiring with me.

703
00:40:37,535 --> 00:40:40,670
You remember that,
uh, meeting you had

704
00:40:40,739 --> 00:40:42,505
in that fancy restaurant?

705
00:40:42,574 --> 00:40:45,975
If you don't, I'm sure she'll
come up here on the stand

706
00:40:46,044 --> 00:40:47,243
and remind you.

707
00:40:47,312 --> 00:40:48,878
I remember it.

708
00:40:48,946 --> 00:40:53,215
So... you do not deny...

709
00:40:53,284 --> 00:40:56,886
you were a member of
Mr. Brennert's scheme.

710
00:40:58,355 --> 00:40:59,622
No.

711
00:40:59,691 --> 00:41:00,901
Actually,

712
00:41:00,925 --> 00:41:03,359
it was Joseph Chan's
scheme, wasn't it?

713
00:41:03,428 --> 00:41:04,527
He came to you first.

714
00:41:04,595 --> 00:41:05,962
He needed you...

715
00:41:06,031 --> 00:41:09,632
to help persuade the
mayor the new expressway

716
00:41:09,700 --> 00:41:11,678
should go through
the industrial section

717
00:41:11,702 --> 00:41:13,536
of the West Side, rather than

718
00:41:13,604 --> 00:41:17,273
through the predominantly
black residential section

719
00:41:17,341 --> 00:41:18,586
on the South Side.

720
00:41:18,610 --> 00:41:19,890
At least...

721
00:41:19,944 --> 00:41:22,111
that's what you
told Mrs. Barelli.

722
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:25,514
Yes, but none of this has
anything whatsoever to do

723
00:41:25,583 --> 00:41:27,583
with the matter
before this court,

724
00:41:27,652 --> 00:41:31,020
which is whether or not
your client is guilty of murder.

725
00:41:31,089 --> 00:41:32,521
I have to concur, Your Honor.

726
00:41:32,590 --> 00:41:34,602
This line of questioning
is irrational and distracting.

727
00:41:34,626 --> 00:41:37,260
Uh, Your Honor, may I just
take a different approach?

728
00:41:37,328 --> 00:41:39,228
Proceed. Thank you.

729
00:41:40,398 --> 00:41:44,334
Do you know a fella
named Eddie Lomax?

730
00:41:44,402 --> 00:41:47,603
Only through conversations
I've had with you.

731
00:41:47,672 --> 00:41:49,784
The police extradited him

732
00:41:49,808 --> 00:41:53,109
from Texas to charge him
with Joseph Chan's murder

733
00:41:53,178 --> 00:41:56,312
and to question him
about the mayor's murder.

734
00:41:56,380 --> 00:41:58,915
And knowing how fast news
travels around the courthouse,

735
00:41:58,983 --> 00:42:01,261
I figured you had heard
about it. Mr. Matlock,

736
00:42:01,285 --> 00:42:03,385
I am too busy to
listen to gossip.

737
00:42:03,454 --> 00:42:05,666
All right, let's forget
about Eddie Lomax

738
00:42:05,690 --> 00:42:06,722
for the moment.

739
00:42:06,791 --> 00:42:09,225
Do you know who Jimmy Dolan is?

740
00:42:10,395 --> 00:42:12,761
I'm sorry, I've
never heard of him.

741
00:42:14,666 --> 00:42:17,432
Before you were a judge,

742
00:42:17,501 --> 00:42:21,103
before you were
on the city council,

743
00:42:21,172 --> 00:42:23,605
before you were
a public defender,

744
00:42:23,675 --> 00:42:25,374
you were a what?

745
00:42:25,442 --> 00:42:28,077
A police officer.
A police officer.

746
00:42:28,146 --> 00:42:30,090
And back when you
were a police officer,

747
00:42:30,114 --> 00:42:33,015
there was a young man
named Jimmy Dolan...

748
00:42:33,084 --> 00:42:37,019
who was arrested on
crimes ranging from assault

749
00:42:37,088 --> 00:42:38,955
to grand theft,

750
00:42:39,023 --> 00:42:42,258
37 times, and
never went to trial.

751
00:42:42,327 --> 00:42:43,892
Never prosecuted.

752
00:42:43,962 --> 00:42:45,594
Now do you remember?

753
00:42:45,663 --> 00:42:47,730
No.

754
00:42:47,799 --> 00:42:51,467
Well, let me read you...

755
00:42:51,535 --> 00:42:53,870
what some of the
arresting officers

756
00:42:53,938 --> 00:42:56,773
had to say back then.

757
00:42:56,841 --> 00:43:01,543
This one, uh, is by
Detective Ted Withers.

758
00:43:01,612 --> 00:43:04,446
May 17, 1974.

759
00:43:04,515 --> 00:43:08,851
"Released Jimmy Dolan,
dropped grand theft auto charge

760
00:43:08,920 --> 00:43:11,821
at Stuart Franklin's request."

761
00:43:11,890 --> 00:43:16,960
Detective Sergeant Arthur
Weingarten, November 14,

762
00:43:17,028 --> 00:43:18,528
1976.

763
00:43:18,596 --> 00:43:21,463
"Surefire case against
Jimmy Dolan dropped,

764
00:43:21,533 --> 00:43:24,166
re: Sergeant Stuart Franklin."

765
00:43:25,270 --> 00:43:28,271
Sergeant Stuart Franklin.

766
00:43:28,340 --> 00:43:31,274
That was you, wasn't it?

767
00:43:31,343 --> 00:43:33,142
Yes.

768
00:43:36,080 --> 00:43:39,014
Jimmy Dolan was
your snitch, wasn't he?

769
00:43:39,083 --> 00:43:41,451
Yes.

770
00:43:41,519 --> 00:43:44,887
He did favors for you,
you did favors for him.

771
00:43:44,956 --> 00:43:47,022
It was so long ago.

772
00:43:47,091 --> 00:43:49,759
He recruited Eddie
Lomax for you, didn't he?

773
00:43:49,828 --> 00:43:51,839
No. Mayor Barelli was about

774
00:43:51,863 --> 00:43:54,530
to expose you as
the Judas you are,

775
00:43:54,599 --> 00:43:56,332
not only for betraying him,

776
00:43:56,401 --> 00:43:59,001
but for betraying members
of the black community

777
00:43:59,070 --> 00:44:00,536
who would have benefited

778
00:44:00,605 --> 00:44:04,384
had that expressway been
built through their district.

779
00:44:04,408 --> 00:44:05,875
So you had Jimmy Dolan

780
00:44:05,944 --> 00:44:08,911
hire someone to
assassinate the mayor

781
00:44:08,980 --> 00:44:11,725
and Joseph Chan, didn't he? No!

782
00:44:11,749 --> 00:44:14,316
And that man was
Eddie Lomax. None of this

783
00:44:14,385 --> 00:44:16,163
is true! Once the police

784
00:44:16,187 --> 00:44:18,654
got hold of Eddie Lomax,

785
00:44:18,723 --> 00:44:22,525
you asked Jimmy Dolan
to do one last favor for you,

786
00:44:22,593 --> 00:44:24,104
didn't you? No.

787
00:44:24,128 --> 00:44:26,262
You had him stab

788
00:44:26,330 --> 00:44:31,033
Eddie Lomax to
death in his cell.

789
00:44:31,102 --> 00:44:33,469
That's right.

790
00:44:33,538 --> 00:44:40,610
Eddie Lomax is dead...
and he was killed...

791
00:44:40,678 --> 00:44:46,916
by a man... who was
hired... by this man.

792
00:44:51,122 --> 00:44:52,888
Coincidence.

793
00:44:52,957 --> 00:44:55,158
It's mere coincidence!

794
00:44:55,226 --> 00:44:57,527
You have not proved one thing.

795
00:44:57,595 --> 00:44:59,428
Maybe not.

796
00:44:59,497 --> 00:45:02,364
And I don't really have to.

797
00:45:02,433 --> 00:45:05,434
But I'm pretty sure I
have demonstrated

798
00:45:05,503 --> 00:45:07,035
that someone other

799
00:45:07,105 --> 00:45:09,372
than my client had
a damn good reason

800
00:45:09,441 --> 00:45:12,207
to kill Mayor Barelli.

801
00:45:14,011 --> 00:45:18,947
That someone
hired... someone else...

802
00:45:19,016 --> 00:45:21,383
to actually commit the crime,

803
00:45:21,452 --> 00:45:23,519
so the first someone

804
00:45:23,588 --> 00:45:26,188
might escape detection.

805
00:45:27,625 --> 00:45:30,326
As far as my client's concerned,

806
00:45:30,395 --> 00:45:35,531
it's called...
"reasonable doubt."

807
00:45:35,599 --> 00:45:40,903
And so far as proof,
I met Jimmy Dolan.

808
00:45:40,972 --> 00:45:43,239
Tall drink of water.

809
00:45:43,308 --> 00:45:46,175
And I'm sure, when
the good officers

810
00:45:46,244 --> 00:45:50,846
of the Chicago police
department talk with him for awhile,

811
00:45:50,915 --> 00:45:53,115
he'll get a little shorter.

812
00:45:55,853 --> 00:45:57,787
Nothing further.

813
00:46:05,997 --> 00:46:09,498
We, the jury, find the
defendant not guilty.

814
00:46:09,567 --> 00:46:10,844
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

815
00:46:10,868 --> 00:46:12,768
This court is adjourned.

816
00:46:14,171 --> 00:46:15,571
Pretty good system, huh?

817
00:46:15,640 --> 00:46:17,283
Better than I thought.

818
00:46:17,307 --> 00:46:18,619
Thank you. Okay.

819
00:46:18,643 --> 00:46:20,320
- Congratulations.
- Hey, listen,

820
00:46:20,344 --> 00:46:23,913
if you ever find yourself
in this situation again,

821
00:46:23,981 --> 00:46:25,848
don't yell at the judge.

822
00:46:28,318 --> 00:46:29,598
See ya.

823
00:46:31,422 --> 00:46:33,121
Dinner tonight?

824
00:46:33,190 --> 00:46:35,791
Oh... I have to pack.

825
00:46:35,860 --> 00:46:37,192
Oh.

826
00:46:38,362 --> 00:46:39,929
I have a story to write.

827
00:46:39,997 --> 00:46:41,196
Oh...

828
00:46:47,572 --> 00:46:49,692
Well.

829
00:46:50,641 --> 00:46:52,407
Well what?

830
00:46:52,476 --> 00:46:54,955
I owe you title to my house.

831
00:46:54,979 --> 00:46:56,244
Oh.

832
00:46:57,414 --> 00:46:59,414
Well, that's okay.

833
00:46:59,483 --> 00:47:02,985
It's probably somewhere I
wouldn't want to live, anyway.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
